WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.530
Find one phone number in a city's

2
00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:05.370
phone book of a million names.

3
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.120
Page by page?

4
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.320
Or open it in the middle?

5
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.150
Today: why a database finds one

6
00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.630
row among millions so fast.

7
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.400
Without help, a database reads row by row

8
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.260
until it finds what you asked for.

9
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.900
On average, that's half the table: five

10
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.220
hundred thousand looks for a million rows.

11
00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.060
But if the ids are sorted, you

12
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.120
can halve the search.

13
00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.490
Look at the middle: too big?

14
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.420
Throw away the top half.

15
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:38.650
Too small?

16
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.880
Throw away the bottom.

17
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.620
Each look halves what's left.

18
00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.430
An index is a sorted copy of one

19
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.790
column, with a pointer to each row.

20
00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.010
For a million rows, halving takes about

21
00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.410
twenty looks, not half a million.

22
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.530
Here's binary search in Python.

23
00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.210
lo and hi mark the part that's left.

24
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.640
Each time round, look at the

25
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.760
middle, and move lo or hi past it.

26
00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:09.350
Quick check!

27
00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.590
A thousand sorted ids.

28
00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:13.940
About how many looks does

29
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.580
halving need to find one?

30
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.160
About ten: two to the power of

31
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.140
ten is just over a thousand.

32
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.180
A thousand times more rows, a

33
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.660
million, needs only about twenty.

34
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.220
Indexes aren't free.

35
00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.570
Each one takes space, and every new

36
00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.110
row must be added to every index.

37
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.830
So index the columns you

38
00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.770
search by, not every column.

39
00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.950
In SQL, one line makes an index.

40
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.250
Real databases use a tree that works like

41
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.970
halving, with many branches, so even a

42
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.090
billion rows take only a few steps.

43
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.400
In the app, watch one lookup both ways,

44
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.360
then grow the table to a million rows.

45
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.500
Without an index, about half a million

46
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.400
looks each; with one, about twenty.

47
00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:08.070
Your turn.

48
00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.920
Why not put an index on every column?

49
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.050
Each one costs space and slows every

50
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.670
insert, so index what you search by.

51
00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.460
Remember: row by row takes half the table.

52
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.710
A sorted index halves the search each

53
00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.510
time: about twenty looks for a million.

54
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.520
And indexes trade space for speed.

55
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.700
Try it tomorrow: play “Guess my number”

56
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.500
from one to a hundred with a friend.

57
00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.470
Always guess the middle.

58
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.620
Can you always win in seven guesses?

59
00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.050
Why seven?
